While Not A True Sequel to 'Alan Wake', Its Merely A Spin-Off But Its The Lesser of Two Evils. It Shines In Areas Where The Original Couldn't. That's Just Scratching The Surface, You Have To Play It For The Full Effect! Here's What I Loved and Not-So Liked....
The writer Alan Wake is still in search of his wife Alice. Only this time, he deals with a doppelganger of himself, 'Mr Scratch'. He constantly traps Alan in a time loop, so until he resolves it, he will face defeat over and over. There are also others in the same predicament as Alan, whom also had encounters one way or another with the mystery villain. Will he break or prevail?
(a) Plot Thickens;
Story's just as mind blowing, despite it being cliched and borrowing (<coughs> robbing) inspiration from other works (what game or art form doesn't?!)
(b) Characters;
This time around, there are more interesting individuals- 'Emma the Hippie', 'Dr Meadows the Skeptic' and 'Serena the Tripping Chick'. Its funny to witness their reaction to the time loop. Voice acting was comparable, if not better than its predecessor's. But WHERE'S BARRY?! Not to forget Alan's wardrobe change (Casual!)

(c) Face of Evil;
Graphics look a tad improved. Most certainly due to the smaller scale of the environment. I have no complaints.
(d) Pieces of The Puzzle;
Manuscripts, radio shows, TV recordings never fail to provide interesting trivia.
(e) Sinister Makeover;
HUD primarily and the way manuscripts are presented, are sufficient to repackage the experience anew.
(f) Grayed Out;
Tweaks to add challenge to those 'difficulty chasers' such as yours truly. Pointing the flashlight on enemies without focusing won't deplete their darkness anymore. The number of flares, flashbangs have tinier quota.
(g) Difficulty;
The game features 'NORMAL' as its chillest and 'NIGHTMARE' its toughest. No 'EASY' because, the experience is made to reflect that of reality's. (Nice move)
(h) A Killer Concert;
I've felt the soundtrack really brought out the intensity of the gameplay and carried the glory. What with the heavy Rock and Roll tracks.
(i) Arcade Action;
Its a nice touch, kinda like 'The Mercenaries' from the Resident Evil Series. Fight, dodge and run.. Finally repeat.
(i) Light-Hearted Puzzles;
Although well-designed- 'Manuscripts to Match The Scene'. Not much thought was asked of the players and were very scarce to even count as 'puzzles'.
(ii) No Longer Black;
The game is a teensy bit difficult but the changes they made to the gameplay; regenerating ammo boxes, Alan being able to sprint nearly three times as long (did he practice for marathons?!) than his counterpart in the previous game, manuscripts being pinpointed on the map as '?' (Yes, they can be turned off) really made guessing where they were while getting lost in the darkness a cakewalk.
This was not the case in the first installment (truly SADISTIC when put side-by-side to this) I only died once or twice the whole duration of the game.
(iii) Spotlight Off The Horror;
Pathetically weak and the darkness didn't seem as distressing, now light shades here and there. Whereas in the previous game, it was permeating and suffocating.
(iv) Monster Designs;
The spiders were ludicrous. (The bird-man and deformed circular saw hillbilly were the main attraction, if put to better and flattering circumstances)
With That Said, Its Still Enjoyable and Its Worth The Price. (Its Sad, Its No Longer Sold On Steam...)
Once Again, This Game Really Speaks To Me and Pushes My Buttons... In A Good Way.
8/10

